---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Start Here" description="Three possible ways into the site, depending on what interests you" lang="en">
  <p class="eyebrow">Not sure where to begin?</p>
  <h1>Start Here</h1>

  <p>
    There are a few different layers of content here — practice,
    learning, and personal writing. None of them need to be read in any
    particular order, so here are three possible entry points, depending
    on what's pulling you right now.
  </p>

  <section class="entry">
    <h2>I want to start practicing</h2>
    <p>
      The simplest place to start is <a href="/en/practices/zazen-shikantaza/">zazen shikantaza</a> —
      the central practice this whole site is built around, sitting with
      no external goal to achieve. If static sitting doesn't speak to
      you as much, <a href="/en/practices/inner-walk/">Inner Walk</a> and
      <a href="/en/practices/wandering/">Wandering</a> are two other
      routes toward the same thing, through walking instead.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/en/practices/">← All practices</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="entry">
    <h2>I want to understand Buddhism better</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/en/inspirations/dogen/">Dogen</a> is a good place to
      start if you're interested in the source — he's the one who
      brought Soto Zen to Japan, along with a core conceptual shift in
      how practice is understood. <a href="/en/inspirations/alan-watts/">Alan Watts</a>
      goes the opposite direction — he's the one who brought Zen to a
      wide Western audience, in accessible, non-religious language. And
      in <a href="/en/retreats/tushita/">my first retreat at Tushita</a>
      you can read what a first encounter with Buddhism actually looked
      like, not just in theory.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/en/inspirations/">← All inspirations</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="entry">
    <h2>I want to read about the path</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/en/lessons/">The lessons</a> are the most personal
      writing on the site — real moments from my own practice, not
      always with an answer at the end. You can start with
      <a href="/en/lessons/2026-08-03-the-question-that-stayed/">The Question That Stayed With Me</a>,
      or just browse the <a href="/en/lessons/">lesson list</a> and see
      what draws you in. <a href="/en/retreats/">The retreats</a> and
      <a href="/en/poems/">the poems</a> are two other routes to the
      same place, from different angles.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p><a href="/en/">← Back to homepage</a></p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .entry {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--stone-line);
  }
  .entry:first-of-type {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }
  .entry h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
